\ ff64.help — help for words specific to the x86-64 port
\ These words do not exist in the original i386 FreeForth.
\ Format: entry lines start at column 0; continuation lines start with space.

d@ ( addr -- n ) fetch 64-bit (double cell) value from memory
  On i386 FreeForth, @ fetches 32 bits (one cell).
  On x86-64, @ already fetches 64 bits, so d@ is equivalent to @.
  Provided for clarity when 64-bit intent matters.

d! ( n addr -- ) store 64-bit (double cell) value to memory
  On i386 FreeForth, ! stores 32 bits. On x86-64, ! stores 64 bits.
  d! is equivalent to ! but documents 64-bit intent.

d, ( n -- ) compile a 64-bit value at here and advance here by 8
  On i386, , compiles 32 bits. On x86-64, , compiles 32 bits (for opcodes).
  d, compiles a full 64-bit value (addresses, pointers).

w, ( n -- ) compile a 16-bit value at here and advance here by 2
  Useful for emitting 16-bit opcode fragments in inline code generators.

d@` compile-time macro for d@ (inline code generation)
d!` compile-time macro for d! (inline code generation)
d,` compile-time macro for d, (inline code generation)
2dupd!` compile-time macro for 2dup d! (inline code generation)
tuckd!` compile-time macro for tuck d! (inline code generation)

8+` compile-time macro: add 8 to TOS (one cell width on x86-64)
  The x86-64 equivalent of i386's 4+` (cell+).

8-` compile-time macro: subtract 8 from TOS
  The x86-64 equivalent of i386's 4-`.

>cs ( n -- ) push a value onto the compile-time stack
  Used by START/ENTER/BREAK/END structured loops to save
  forward-reference addresses for later resolution.

cs> ( -- n ) pop a value from the compile-time stack
  Used by END to resolve break addresses saved by BREAK.

ds ( -- ) display the data stack contents (runtime version of .s`)
  Prints the current stack depth and all values.
  .s` is the compile-time equivalent (used in prompts).

h.name ( hdr -- ) print the name field of a dictionary header
  Given a header address, prints the word's name.

h.next ( hdr -- hdr' ) advance to the next dictionary header
  Computes the address of the following header by adding
  the name length + fixed header overhead.

loadfile ( addr len -- ) load and compile a Forth source file
  Opens the named file, reads it into filebuf, and passes
  the contents through the compiler. Saves and restores
  the current parsing state (>in, tp).

ff_argc ( -- addr ) variable holding the command-line argument count
  Set by assembly at startup from the C argc value.

ff_argv ( -- addr ) variable holding the pointer to argv array
  Set by assembly at startup. Each entry is a pointer to a
  NUL-terminated string.

helpbuf ( -- addr ) 128KB buffer used by the help system
  Used by lib/help64.ff to read ff.help into memory.

xfp ( -- addr ) variable: exception frame pointer for catch/throw
  Points to the current catch frame on the return stack.
  Zero when no catch frame is active.

stderr ( -- 2 ) file descriptor constant for standard error

cond.` compile-time macro: convert stack boolean to FLAGS condition
  Emits 0- drop 0<> — tests TOS, drops it, sets FLAGS.
  Used by IF. WHILE. UNTIL. (dotted conditionals) to bridge
  stack booleans to FreeForth's FLAGS-based control flow.

LOOP` compile-time macro for counted loops
  Used with TIMES: TIMES ... LOOP iterates a counted loop.
  LOOP decrements the counter, branches back if nonzero.

dlsetup ( -- ) open libc.so.6 and cache the handle in variable libc
  Called automatically at boot. Safe to call multiple times
  (returns immediately if already loaded).

libc. ( <name> -- ) compile-time macro: resolve and call a libc function

needexec ( addr len -- ) load a file and execute its last definition
  Calls needed (with double-load guard), then executes the most
  recently defined word via H@ @ execute. Used for lazy loading
  of library files (e.g., help`, see`).

pad ( -- addr ) scratch buffer address, 256 bytes above here
  Returns here + 256. This is a temporary workspace safe from
  ongoing compilation at here. The address changes whenever here
  changes — do not hold across definitions. ANS-compatible.
  see also: here allot

cmove ( src dst n -- ) copy n bytes from src to dst (low to high)
  Forward byte copy. Use for non-overlapping copies or when
  dst < src. For overlapping copies where dst > src, use cmove>.
  see also: cmove> move place

cmove> ( src dst n -- ) copy n bytes from src to dst (high to low)
  Reverse byte copy. Safe for overlapping regions where dst > src.
  Used by _hidepvt compaction to shift header chain entries.
  see also: cmove move

move ( src dst n -- ) smart copy: automatically selects direction
  If dst < src, uses cmove (forward). Otherwise uses cmove> (reverse).
  Safe for all overlapping copies. ANS-compatible.
  see also: cmove cmove>

place ( src count dst -- ) copy counted string to destination
  Copies count bytes from src to dst. Equivalent to cmove but
  with arguments reordered for string operations.
  see also: cmove move

fill ( addr n byte -- ) fill n bytes at addr with byte
  see also: erase

erase ( addr n -- ) fill n bytes at addr with zero
  Equivalent to: 0 fill
  see also: fill

count ( addr -- addr+1 byte ) read counted string header
  ANS name for c@+. Reads byte at addr, increments addr.
  see also: c@+

bounds ( addr n -- addr addr+n ) convert address+length to address pair
  Commonly used with BEGIN...WHILE loops to iterate over a range.

within ( n lo hi -- ; flags ) test if lo <= n < hi
  Sets FLAGS (not stack boolean). Use with IF/WHILE/UNTIL.
  Returns nzTRUE if in range, zFALSE otherwise.
  see also: < > = u< u>

zt ( addr len -- addr ) zero-terminate a string
  Writes a NUL byte at addr+len. Returns addr.

words ( -- ) list all words in the dictionary
  Walks the header chain and prints each word's name.

dump ( addr n -- ) hex dump of n bytes starting at addr
  Prints 16 bytes per line with address headers.

!^ ( new-target xt -- ) set vector target
  Writes new-target into the vector's push operand at xt+1.
  The vector will now jump to new-target when called.
  see also: @^ n^ ^^ x^ :^

@^ ( xt -- target ) read current vector target
  Reads the 32-bit push operand at xt+1.
  see also: !^ n^ ^^ x^

n^ ( xt -- ) nop a vector (make it do nothing)
  Sets the vector's target to xt+5 (the ret instruction).
  The vector becomes: push xt+5; ret → jumps to ret → returns.
  see also: !^ @^ ^^ x^

x^ ( xt -- ) execute a vector's body directly
  Pushes xt+6 onto the return stack, causing execution to
  continue at the vector's default body (skipping the push/ret).
  see also: !^ @^ n^ ^^

^^ ( xt -- ) reset vector to its default body
  Writes xt+6 (body start) into the push operand at xt+1.
  Restores the vector to its original behavior.
  see also: !^ @^ n^ x^

:^ ( <name> -- ) define a vector (redirectable word)
  Creates a word with a push/ret preamble: 68 <target32> C3.
  The body follows at xt+6. The push operand can be rewritten
  by !^ n^ ^^ to redirect or disable the vector.
  see also: !^ @^ n^ ^^ x^

quit ( -- ) reset _top to default and restart the REPL
  Resets the _top vector to its original body, then calls it.

2swap ( a b c d -- c d a b ) swap the top two pairs
  Defined as: rot >r rot r>
  Also available as compile-time macro 2swap`.

max ( a b -- max ) return the larger of two values
min ( a b -- min ) return the smaller of two values

nop ( -- ) do nothing
  Empty word, used as placeholder for vector targets.

[os] ( -- 1 ) compile-time constant: operating system flag
  Returns 1 for Linux. A future Windows port would return 0.
  Used for conditional compilation: [os] [IF] ... [THEN]

argv ( n -- addr len ) get nth command-line argument as string
  Returns address and length of the NUL-terminated argument.
  see also: argc ff_argc ff_argv

needed ( addr len -- ) load file unless already loaded
  Appends ` to filename, checks if that word exists. If found,
  the file was already loaded — returns. Otherwise searches
  FFPATH (lib/64:lib:.) for the file and loads it via loadfile.
  Absolute paths (/...) and relative paths (./...) bypass FFPATH.
  see also: needexec needs` loadfile openlib

needs` ( <filename> -- ) load file by name (compile-time)
  Semicolons first, then parses filename and calls needed.
  see also: needed needexec

openlib ( addr len -- addr' len' | -1 -1 ) search FFPATH for a file
  Tries each directory in FFPATH (lib/64, lib, .) to find the file.
  Returns the full path and length on success, or -1 -1 on failure.
  Paths starting with / or . are returned unchanged (no search).
  Called by needed; not normally used directly.
  see also: needed

doargv ( -- ) process command-line arguments
  Evaluates all arguments after the program name as Forth.
  Called by _postboot during normal boot.

-f` ( <filename> -- ) load a source file (compile-time)
  Loads the named file via needed. If the file defines "main",
  switches to turnkey mode: _top becomes _main, _postboot
  is nop'd, and argc/argv belong to main.
  see also: needed mainxt

++` compile-time macro: increment variable (peephole optimization)
  Rewrites a preceding variable fetch into an in-place increment.
  Usage: variable x  ... x ++
  see also: --`

--` compile-time macro: decrement variable (peephole optimization)
  Rewrites a preceding variable fetch into an in-place decrement.
  see also: ++`

features ( -- addr ) buffer holding loaded feature names
  Space-separated list of features. Printed by -v.

-v ( -- ) display loaded features
  Prints "features:" followed by the feature list.

eval ( addr len -- ) compile and run Forth source
  Sets up the input buffer to the given string and calls the
  compiler. Does not auto-execute trailing anonymous code.
  see also: eval.

eval. ( addr len -- ) compile and auto-execute Forth source
  Like eval but calls _auto at the end, which executes any
  trailing anonymous code (unless noauto is set).
  see also: eval

syscall ( args... #args syscall# -- ior ) generic Linux syscall
  Same Forth interface as i386 fflinio.asm. Push args so that
  the last-pushed arg maps to the first kernel register (rdi).
  For write(fd, buf, count): count buf fd 3 1 syscall
  NOTE: x86-64 syscall numbers differ from i386!
  (e.g., read=0, write=1, open=2, close=3, exit=60)
  Up to 6 args supported (rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9).
  see also: read write openr openw close exit

SEGVhndlr ( -- ) signal handler for SIGSEGV
  Throws "SEGV caught" via !" (inline error + throw).
  Installed via libc sigaction(2) during boot (SEGVthrow).
  Replaces the assembly-level fatal handler with a recoverable
  one — the REPL's catch frame catches the throw and continues.
  see also: SEGVthrow catch throw

SEGVthrow ( -- ) install SEGV handler via libc sigaction
  Calls sigaction(SIGSEGV, &SEGVact, NULL) to install SEGVhndlr.
  Called automatically during boot. Can be called again to
  re-install the handler (e.g., after a fork).
  see also: SEGVhndlr

fixup ( addr len -- ) self-patching libc symbol resolver
  Used in hidden definitions: :. _xxx "symbol" fixup ;
  On first call: resolves "symbol" via dlsym, allocates an
  11-byte trampoline (movabs rbx,imm64; ret), and patches
  the caller's call instruction to point to the trampoline.
  Subsequent calls go directly to the trampoline.
  IMPORTANT: the hidden definition MUST end with ; for tail-call.
  Loaded from: "fixup.ff" needed
  see also: #lib #fun #call libc@

strerror ( errno -- ) print error message for errno
  Takes a negative errno (as returned by FreeForth syscalls),
  negates it, and calls C strerror() via fixup to print the
  corresponding error message.
  Example: -13 strerror  →  "Permission denied"
  Loaded from: "ior.ff" needed
  see also: ?ior ?ior. ior

ior ( -- addr ) variable holding last I/O result
  Loaded from: "ior.ff" needed
  see also: ?ior ?ior. strerror

?ior ( n -- n ) store I/O result
  Stores n in the ior variable. Does not consume n.
  Loaded from: "ior.ff" needed
  see also: ior ?ior. strerror

?ior. ( n -- n ) store and optionally display I/O error
  Stores n in ior. If n looks like an errno (dup $FF | -1 <>),
  drops it silently. Otherwise calls strerror and throws.
  Loaded from: "ior.ff" needed
  see also: ior ?ior strerror

malloc ( size -- addr ) allocate memory
  Calls C malloc() via fixup. Returns address of allocated block.
  Loaded from: "malloc.ff" needed
  see also: free

free ( addr -- ) release allocated memory
  Calls C free() via fixup.
  Loaded from: "malloc.ff" needed
  see also: malloc

getenv ( addr len -- addr len ) read environment variable
  Returns the value string of the named env var.
  Example: "HOME" getenv type
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed
  see also: getpid system shell cd !!

getpid ( -- pid ) current process ID
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed

getppid ( -- pid ) parent process ID
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed

system ( addr len -- result ) execute shell command
  Passes null-terminated string to C system().
  FreeForth strings use _ for space: "ls_-la" system
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed
  see also: shell !!

shell ( -- ) start interactive shell
  Starts $SHELL (or /bin/sh). Return to FreeForth with 'exit'.
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed

cd ( addr len -- ) change directory
  Uses chdir syscall (80).
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed

!! ( -- ) re-execute last shell command
  Loaded from: "shell.ff" needed

lseek ( whence offset fd -- offset ) reposition file offset
  x86-64 syscall 8.
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed

ioctl ( arg request fd -- result ) device control
  x86-64 syscall 16.
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed

select ( timeout exceptfds writefds readfds nfds -- result )
  Monitor file descriptors. x86-64 syscall 23.
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed

stat ( addr len statbuf -- result ) get file status
  Calls stat syscall. statbuf must be ≥144 bytes.
  st_size is at offset 48 in the struct.
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed
  see also: mkst st.size

mkst ( name -- ) compile-time: create a 144-byte stat buffer
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed

st.size ( -- 48 ) offset of st_size in struct stat
  Loaded from: "fileops.ff" needed

cls ( -- ) clear screen (ANSI escape)
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

home ( -- ) move cursor to 0,0
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

atxy ( col row -- ) position cursor
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

atx ( col -- ) position cursor on current row
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

color ( n -- ) set ANSI color/attribute
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed
  see also: normal bold nocolor foreground background

key? ( -- ; zFALSE ) test if key is available on stdin
  Uses select to check stdin readability.
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

ekey ( -- c ) read one key in raw mode
  Puts terminal in raw mode, reads one byte, restores.
  Loaded from: "console.ff" needed

.now ( -- ) display current date and time
  Example output: sat 2026-2-28_4:01:19
  Loaded from: "time.ff" needed
  see also: .d .wd .dt .t ms@ ms

ms@ ( -- ms ) current time in milliseconds
  Uses clock_gettime (syscall 228).
  Loaded from: "time.ff" needed
  see also: ms

ms ( n -- ) delay n milliseconds
  Uses nanosleep (syscall 35).
  Loaded from: "time.ff" needed
  see also: ms@
